{{ comp.tabbedCode({ tabs: [
  {
    label: "A checkout with a real refund path",
    lang: "ts",
    code: "import { defineSaga, sagaCompensate, send } from '@netscript/plugin-sagas-core';\n\ntype Checkout = { status: 'pending' | 'paid' };\ntype Failure = { orderId: string; reason: string };\n\nexport const checkout = defineSaga('checkout')\n  .state<Checkout>({ status: 'pending' })\n  .on<'payment.captured', { orderId: string }>(\n    'payment.captured',\n    (saga, event) => {\n      saga.state.status = 'paid';\n      return [send('inventory.reserve', { orderId: event.payload.orderId })];\n    },\n  )\n  .on<'inventory.failed', Failure>('inventory.failed', (_saga, event) => [\n    sagaCompensate(\n      { type: 'payment.captured', payload: { orderId: event.payload.orderId } },\n      event.payload.reason,\n    ),\n  ])\n  .compensate<'payment.captured', { orderId: string }>(\n    'payment.captured',\n    (_saga, event) => [send('payment.refund', { orderId: event.payload.orderId })],\n  )\n  .build();"
  }
] }) }}

<p>
When <code>inventory.failed</code> arrives, the forward handler emits
<code>sagaCompensate(...)</code> for <code>payment.captured</code>. The runtime routes that cascade
to the matching compensation handler, which emits <code>payment.refund</code>. A configured saga
store records transitions between messages, so restart recovery is a runtime property—not
something this builder declaration performs by itself.
<a href="{{ '/durable-workflows/' |> url }}">See the durability model</a>.
</p>

